# L4DEL overlay for the FLT project. Written from the pinned source.
# IMPORTANT: the project is unfinished. Several upstream results are `sorry` or `knownin1980s`.

@file FLT/Proof.lean
@decl flt
-- L4DEL: title: Fermat's Last Theorem, conditional on unfinished work
-- L4DEL: intuition: This is Mathlib's FermatLastTheorem, obtained by chaining the reductions B4 to B3 to B2 to B1; it is not yet a complete proof, because it rests on the unproved theorem B4_proof.
-- L4DEL: step: The proof term is B1_proof, and B1 is defined to be exactly FermatLastTheorem.
-- L4DEL: step: B1_proof comes from B2_proof by the lemma that FLT follows from the case of primes at least 5.
-- L4DEL: step: B2_proof comes from B3_proof, which comes from B4_proof by the Mazur step.
-- L4DEL: step: B4_proof is currently `sorry`, so the theorem depends on `sorryAx`. The file FermatsLastTheorem.lean records that the axioms of the final statement include sorryAx and the project axiom knownin1980s.
-- L4DEL: prereq: FLT.Bosses.B1, FLT.Bosses.B3_implies_B2, FLT.Bosses.B4_implies_B3, FLT.Bosses.B4_proof
-- L4DEL: see: PNat.pow_add_pow_ne_pow_of_FermatLastTheorem
-- L4DEL: term: sorryAx = the axiom behind Lean's `sorry`; a theorem whose axioms include it has an unfinished proof somewhere below it

@file FLT/Proof.lean
@decl FLT.Bosses.B4_proof
-- L4DEL: title: The remaining spine gap: the p-torsion is reducible (unproved)
-- L4DEL: intuition: This is the deep core of the modularity-lifting argument, stated as B4 (the p-torsion of the Frey curve is a reducible Galois representation), and in this version of the repository its proof is just `sorry`.
-- L4DEL: step: There is no proof to describe: the body is `sorry`, so B4 is an unproved assumption of the whole development.
-- L4DEL: step: In the file's own outline, B5 and B6 (lifting hardly ramified representations and compatible families) are sketched in a comment only.
-- L4DEL: prereq: FLT.Bosses.B4
-- L4DEL: see: FLT.Bosses.B4_implies_B3

@file FLT/Proof.lean
@decl FLT.Bosses.B4_implies_B3
-- L4DEL: title: Reducible p-torsion rules out a Frey package
-- L4DEL: intuition: If the p-torsion of every Frey curve must be reducible, but Mazur's theorem says it is irreducible, then no Frey package can exist.
-- L4DEL: step: Assume B4 and suppose a Frey package P is given.
-- L4DEL: step: B4 applied to P says the Galois representation on the p-torsion of P's Frey curve is not irreducible.
-- L4DEL: step: FreyPackage.mazur says that same representation is irreducible, giving a contradiction, so the type of Frey packages is empty (B3).
-- L4DEL: prereq: FLT.Bosses.B3, FLT.Bosses.B4, FreyPackage.mazur
-- L4DEL: see: FLT.Bosses.B4_proof

@file FLT/Proof.lean
@decl FLT.Bosses.B3_implies_B2
-- L4DEL: title: No Frey package implies FLT for primes at least 5
-- L4DEL: intuition: A counterexample to FLT at a prime p at least 5 can be normalised into a Frey package, so if none exist there is no counterexample.
-- L4DEL: step: The proof is a direct appeal to FreyPackage.fermatLastTheoremFor_p_ge_5, which does the normalisation (see that lemma).
-- L4DEL: prereq: FLT.Bosses.B2, FLT.Bosses.B3, FreyPackage.fermatLastTheoremFor_p_ge_5
-- L4DEL: see: FreyPackage

@file FLT/Basic/Lemmas.lean
@decl FermatLastTheorem.of_p_ge_5
-- L4DEL: title: It suffices to prove FLT for primes at least 5
-- L4DEL: intuition: Any exponent n at least 3 is divisible by 3, by 4, or by a prime at least 5, and FLT for a divisor implies FLT for the multiple.
-- L4DEL: step: Split on the arithmetic fact that n is divisible by 3, or by 4, or by some prime p at least 5.
-- L4DEL: step: If 3 divides n, use Euler's theorem fermatLastTheoremThree from Mathlib and monotonicity along divisibility.
-- L4DEL: step: If 4 divides n, use Fermat's theorem fermatLastTheoremFour from Mathlib in the same way.
-- L4DEL: step: Otherwise a prime p at least 5 divides n and the hypothesis H gives FLT for p, which passes up to n.
-- L4DEL: prereq: Nat.three_dvd_or_four_dvd_or_prime_dvd
-- L4DEL: see: FLT.Bosses.B2_implies_B1

@file FLT/FreyCurve/FreyPackage.lean
@decl FreyPackage
-- L4DEL: title: Frey package
-- L4DEL: intuition: A Frey package bundles a hypothetical counterexample a^p + b^p = c^p, with a prime p at least 5, into normalised data (a coprime to b, a congruent to 3 mod 4, b even) so that the associated elliptic curve is well behaved.
-- L4DEL: step: The structure carries integers a, b, c, all nonzero, and a natural number p that is prime with 5 at most p.
-- L4DEL: step: It carries the equation hFLT, coprimality of a and b, the congruence a = 3 mod 4, and evenness of b.
-- L4DEL: step: Its docstring explains the historical role: Serre (using Mazur) showed the p-torsion of the resulting curve is irreducible, Ribet showed it cannot be (assuming modularity), and Wiles proved modularity.
-- L4DEL: see: FreyPackage.freyCurve, FreyPackage.of_not_FermatLastTheoremFor_p_ge_5
-- L4DEL: term: Frey package = a normalised hypothetical counterexample (a, b, c, p) to Fermat's Last Theorem with p a prime at least 5

@file FLT/FreyCurve/FreyPackage.lean
@decl FreyPackage.fermatLastTheoremFor_p_ge_5
-- L4DEL: title: Empty type of Frey packages gives FLT for p at least 5
-- L4DEL: intuition: By contraposition: a counterexample for some prime p at least 5 would produce a Frey package.
-- L4DEL: step: Assume the type of Frey packages is empty, take a prime p at least 5, and suppose FLT fails for p.
-- L4DEL: step: The earlier lemma of_not_FermatLastTheoremFor_p_ge_5 builds a Frey package from the counterexample.
-- L4DEL: step: That contradicts emptiness.
-- L4DEL: prereq: FreyPackage, FreyPackage.of_not_FermatLastTheoremFor_p_ge_5

@file FLT/FreyCurve/Mazur.lean
@decl FreyPackage.mazur
-- L4DEL: title: Mazur step: the p-torsion of the Frey curve is irreducible (assumed)
-- L4DEL: intuition: The statement is that the Galois representation on the p-torsion of the Frey curve is irreducible, a consequence of Mazur's work as used in Serre's 1987 Duke paper.
-- L4DEL: step: The proof is the project tactic `knownin1980s`, which behaves like `sorry`; it is not a formal proof of the claim.
-- L4DEL: step: The project's own policy (module doc of the tactic) allows it only for results the maintainers believe are provable on paper from literature known before 1990.
-- L4DEL: prereq: FreyPackage, FreyPackage.freyCurve
-- L4DEL: see: FLT.Bosses.B4_implies_B3
-- L4DEL: term: knownin1980s = a project tactic that closes any goal like `sorry`, reserved for results believed to follow from literature known before 1990

