@file FLT/DedekindDomain/FiniteAdeleRing/TensorPi.lean
@decl tensorPiEquivPiTensor'
-- L4DEL: title: Tensoring with a finitely-presented module commutes with arbitrary products
-- L4DEL: intuition: Tensoring with a finitely-presented module commutes with arbitrary products. To prove this, we consider the following commutative diagram. The goal is to show that `i₃` is an isomorphism, which we do using the five lemma: ``` Rᵐ ⊗[R] (Π i, N i) --f₁--> Rⁿ ⊗[R] (Π i, N i) --f₂--> M ⊗[R] (Π i, N i) --f₃--> 0 --f₄--> 0 | | | | | i₁ i₂ i₃ i₄ i₅ | | | | | v v v v v Π i, (Rᵐ ⊗[R] N i) --g₁--> Π i, (Rⁿ ⊗[R] N i) --g₂--> Π i, (M ⊗[R] N i) --g₃--> 0 --g₄--> 0 ```
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/DedekindDomain/FiniteAdeleRing/TensorProduct.lean
@decl IsDedekindDomain.FiniteAdeleRing.TensorProduct.localcomponent
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: localcomponent
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `φ : 𝔸_K^f ⊗[K] V → 𝔸_K^f ⊗[K] V` is `𝔸_K^f`-linear and `p : HeightOneSpectrum (𝓞 K)` then `localcomponent R K V p φ : Kₚ ⊗[K] V →[K] Kₚ ⊗[K] V` is the associated map `φₚ` defined as `Kₚ ⊗[K] V --(single)--> 𝔸_K^f ⊗ V --(φ)--> 𝔸_K^f ⊗ V --(eval)--> Kₚ ⊗ V`. This map morally satisfies `φ = Πₚ φₚ` but because source of φ isn't literally a restricted product we cannot make this assertion.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/DedekindDomain/FiniteAdeleRing/TensorProduct.lean
@decl IsDedekindDomain.FiniteAdeleRing.TensorProduct.localcomponent_apply
