@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/QuadraticTwists.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.not_exists_smul_quadraticTwist_eq
-- L4DEL: title: If `j(E) ∉ {0, 1728}` (so that the only automorphisms of `E` are `±1`) then the quadratic twist is *not* isomorphic to `E` over `K`: twisting by `L/K` is a nontrivial operation
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `j(E) ∉ {0, 1728}` (so that the only automorphisms of `E` are `±1`) then the quadratic twist is *not* isomorphic to `E` over `K`: twisting by `L/K` is a nontrivial operation. This can fail for `j ∈ {0, 1728}`: e.g. for `E : y² = x³ + x` of `j`-invariant `1728` over `K = ℚ(i)`, the quadratic twist by any `L = K(d^{1/2})` with `d ∈ (K^×)⁴ ∖ (K^×)²` is isomorphic to `E` over `K`, because `E` admits the automorphism `(x, y) ↦ (-x, iy)` of order 4.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/QuadraticTwists.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.quadraticTwist
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: quadraticTwist
-- L4DEL: intuition: The quadratic twist of an elliptic curve `E` over `K` by a separable quadratic extension `L/K`: the twist `WeierstrassCurve.quadraticTwistBy` by an arbitrarily chosen generator `θ ∈ L ∖ K`. The twist is independent of this choice up to isomorphism over `K` (`exists_smul_quadraticTwist_eq_quadraticTwistBy`), that is, up to the action of `WeierstrassCurve.VariableChange K`; see `WeierstrassCurve.quadraticTwistOf` for the explicit Weierstrass model. (The separability hypothesis is not used to write down the model, but the twist is only meaningful for separable extensions — see the module docstring.)
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/QuadraticTwists.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.quadraticTwist_of_two_ne_zero
-- L4DEL: title: The classical formula for the quadratic twist away from characteristic 2
-- L4DEL: intuition: The classical formula for the quadratic twist away from characteristic 2. Suppose `char K ≠ 2`, so that after completing the square we may assume `E` has the form `y² = x³ + a₂x² + a₄x + a₆`, and suppose `L = K(α)` where `α² = d` is a nonsquare in `K` (every separable quadratic extension arises this way when `char K ≠ 2`). Then the quadratic twist of `E` by `L` is `y² = x³ + da₂x² + d²a₄x + d³a₆`, up to `K`-isomorphism.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/QuadraticTwists.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.quadraticTwistBy
-- L4DEL: title: The quadratic twist of a Weierstrass curve `E` over `K` by an element `θ` of an extension `L/K`: the twist `WeierstrassCurve.quadraticTwistOf` by the trace and norm of `θ`
-- L4DEL: intuition: The quadratic twist of a Weierstrass curve `E` over `K` by an element `θ` of an extension `L/K`: the twist `WeierstrassCurve.quadraticTwistOf` by the trace and norm of `θ`. This is "the" quadratic twist of `E` by the extension `L/K` whenever `L/K` is separable quadratic and `θ` generates it, i.e. `θ ∉ K`; the choice of generator does not matter, up to isomorphism over `K` (`exists_smul_quadraticTwistBy_eq`).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/QuadraticTwists.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.quadraticTwistOf
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: quadraticTwistOf
-- L4DEL: intuition: The quadratic twist of a Weierstrass curve `E` over `K` by parameters `t`, `n`, to be thought of as the trace and norm of a generator `θ` of a separable quadratic extension `L/K` (see `WeierstrassCurve.quadraticTwistBy`), so that `θ² = tθ - n` and `D := t² - 4n` is the discriminant of the minimal polynomial of `θ`, nonzero exactly when `θ` is separable. The construction: writing the equation of `E` as `y² + A(x)y = f(x)` with `A(x) = a₁x + a₃`, the functions `x` and `Y := (t - 2θ)y - θ·A(x)` on `E` are invariant under the Galois action twisted by the quadratic character of `L/K`, and satisfy `Y² + t·A(x)·Y = D·(y² + A(x)y) - n·A(x)²`; clearing denominators via `(x, Y) ↦ (Dx, DY)` turns this relation into the Weierstrass model below of the twist: `y² + ta₁·xy + Dta₃·y = x³ + (Da₂ - na₁²)·x² + (D²a₄ - 2Dna₁a₃)·x + (D³a₆ - D²na₃²)`. Its discriminant is `D⁶·Δ(E)` (`Δ_quadraticTwistOf`), so the twist of an elliptic curve is elliptic when `D ≠ 0` (`isElliptic_quadraticTwistOf`), with the same `j`-invariant (`j_quadraticTwistOf`). Sanity checks. If `char K ≠ 2` we may take `θ = √d`, so `t = 0`, `n = -d`, `D = 4d`; for `E : y² = x³ + a₂x² + a₄x + a₆` the model is `y² = x³ + 4da₂x² + 16d²a₄x + 64d³a₆`, the classical twist by `4d ≡ d mod (K^×)²`. If `char K = 2` we may take `θ` with `θ² + θ = d` (Artin–Schreier), so `t = 1`, `n = -d`, `D = 1`; for ordinary `E : y² + xy = x³ + a₂x² + a₆` the model is the classical twist `y² + xy = x³ + (a₂ + d)x² + a₆`, and for supersingular `E : y² + a₃y = x³ + a₄x + a₆` it is `y² + a₃y = x³ + a₄x + (a₆ + da₃²)`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/QuadraticTwists.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.quadraticTwistOf_map
-- L4DEL: title: The quadratic twist commutes with a ring homomorphism `f` (in particular with base change): `(E.quadraticTwistOf t n).map f = (E.map f).quadraticTwistOf (f t) (f n)`
-- L4DEL: intuition: The quadratic twist commutes with a ring homomorphism `f` (in particular with base change): `(E.quadraticTwistOf t n).map f = (E.map f).quadraticTwistOf (f t) (f n)`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/QuadraticTwists.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.quadraticTwistPointEquiv
-- L4DEL: title: The isomorphism `Eᴸ(M) ≅ E(M)` on `M`-points, for any field `M` in a tower `K ⊆ L ⊆ M`: the base change to `M` of a choice of isomorphism between `Eᴸ` and `E` over `L`
-- L4DEL: intuition: The isomorphism `Eᴸ(M) ≅ E(M)` on `M`-points, for any field `M` in a tower `K ⊆ L ⊆ M`: the base change to `M` of a choice of isomorphism between `Eᴸ` and `E` over `L`. It is natural in `M` (`quadraticTwistPointEquiv_map`) and transforms the Galois action into the Galois action twisted by the quadratic character of `L/K` (`quadraticTwistPointEquiv_galois`). Like the twist itself, this isomorphism is well defined only up to composition with an `L`-automorphism of `E` — generically, up to sign — and this definition makes an arbitrary but single choice, consistent across all `M`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/QuadraticTwists.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.quadraticTwistPointEquiv_conj
-- L4DEL: title: Special case of `quadraticTwistPointEquiv_galois` over `M = L` itself: the isomorphism `φ : Eᴸ(L) ≅ E(L)` intertwines the action of the nontrivial element `σ ∈ Gal(L/K)` with `-σ`
-- L4DEL: intuition: Special case of `quadraticTwistPointEquiv_galois` over `M = L` itself: the isomorphism `φ : Eᴸ(L) ≅ E(L)` intertwines the action of the nontrivial element `σ ∈ Gal(L/K)` with `-σ`. This is the datum classically used to define the twist by Galois descent.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/QuadraticTwists.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.quadraticTwistPointEquiv_galois
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: quadraticTwistPointEquiv_galois
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Twisting the Galois action.** The Galois action on the points of the quadratic twist is the Galois action on the points of `E`, twisted by the quadratic character of `L/K`: for `σ ∈ Aut(M/K)`, transporting the action of `σ` on `Eᴸ(M)` through `Eᴸ(M) ≅ E(M)` gives `χ(σ)` times the action of `σ` on `E(M)`. Taking `M` to be a separable closure of `K`, this says that the Galois representations attached to `Eᴸ` (e.g. on torsion points) are those attached to `E`, twisted by `χ`. Taking `M = L` and `σ ≠ 1` recovers the classical anti-equivariance of the isomorphism `φ : Eᴸ ≅ E` over `L` (see `quadraticTwistPointEquiv_conj`). Note the statement is unchanged under replacing `φ` by `-φ`, so it does not depend on the sign choice hidden in `quadraticTwistPointEquiv`. Note also that for `σ` fixing `L` pointwise (i.e. `σ ∈ Aut(M/L)`, `χ(σ) = 1`) it is a special case of naturality, via the `L`-algebra map underlying `σ`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/QuadraticTwists.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.quadraticTwistPointEquiv_map
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: quadraticTwistPointEquiv_map
-- L4DEL: intuition: Naturality of `quadraticTwistPointEquiv` in `M`: the isomorphisms on `M`-points over varying `M ⊇ L` are all induced by a single isomorphism of curves over `L`, so they commute with the maps on points induced by any `L`-algebra homomorphism.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/QuadraticTwists.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.quadraticTwistPointEquiv_map_of_not_fixed
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: quadraticTwistPointEquiv_map_of_not_fixed
-- L4DEL: intuition: The **anti-equivariance** underlying `quadraticTwistPointEquiv_galois`: if `σ ∈ Aut(M/K)` does not fix `L` pointwise (`χ(σ) = -1`), then transporting its action through `Eᴸ(M) ≅ E(M)` gives minus its action. This is the point-level shadow of `quadraticTwistVarChange_baseChange_map`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/QuadraticTwists.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.quadraticTwistVarChange
-- L4DEL: title: A choice of change of variables over `L` carrying `E` to its quadratic twist `Eᴸ`
-- L4DEL: intuition: A choice of change of variables over `L` carrying `E` to its quadratic twist `Eᴸ`. Using the explicit isomorphism of `exists_smul_baseChange_and_map_eq` (rather than an arbitrary one) ensures its Galois cocycle is exactly `[-1]` (`quadraticTwistVarChange_map`), unconditionally in `j`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/QuadraticTwists.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.quadraticTwistVarChange_baseChange_map
-- L4DEL: title: The `M`-level form of the twist's defining cocycle: any `σ ∈ Aut(M/K)` not fixing `L` pointwise (i.e
-- L4DEL: intuition: The `M`-level form of the twist's defining cocycle: any `σ ∈ Aut(M/K)` not fixing `L` pointwise (i.e. with `χ(σ) = -1`) conjugates the base change to `M` of `quadraticTwistVarChange` by the automorphism `[-1]` of `E`. This is the base change of `quadraticTwistVarChange_map`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/QuadraticTwists.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.quadraticTwistVarChange_map
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: quadraticTwistVarChange_map
-- L4DEL: intuition: **The defining cocycle of the quadratic twist.** The nontrivial `σ ∈ Gal(L/K)` conjugates the change of variables `quadraticTwistVarChange` (carrying `E` to `Eᴸ`) by the automorphism `[-1]` of `E`. This is the datum expressing that `Eᴸ` is the descent of `E` along the twisted Galois action `σ ↦ (-1) ∘ σ`, and it holds for every `j`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/SplitMultiplicativeReduction.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.baseChange_integralModel_quadraticTwistOf
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: baseChange_integralModel_quadraticTwistOf
-- L4DEL: intuition: The `R`-model twist base-changes to the twist over `K`: for `E` integral over `R`, twisting its integral model by `t, n : R` and base-changing to `K` equals twisting `E` by the images `(algebraMap R K t, algebraMap R K n)`. Together with the coefficient laws this is the bridge from the `K`-twist `E.quadraticTwist L` to a genuine `R`-model whose reduction can be computed.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/SplitMultiplicativeReduction.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.exists_quadraticTwist_hasSplitMultiplicativeReduction
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: exists_quadraticTwist_hasSplitMultiplicativeReduction
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `E` has multiplicative reduction which is not split, then `E` has a quadratic twist with split multiplicative reduction — namely the twist by the unramified quadratic extension of `K`: the reduction of the twist is the same nodal cubic with the Galois action on the two tangent directions at the node twisted into triviality. Mathlib's reduction-type predicates apply to a specific Weierstrass equation and require it to be minimal, while our chosen model `E.quadraticTwist L` of the twist need not be; so the conclusion is phrased via the minimal model `(E.quadraticTwist L).minimal R`. (Being split multiplicative is intrinsic, so any other minimal model would do.) The nonsplitness hypothesis `h` cannot be dropped: if `E` already has split multiplicative reduction then *no* quadratic twist of `E` has split multiplicative reduction, since the unramified quadratic twist has nonsplit multiplicative reduction and ramified quadratic twists have additive reduction.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/SplitMultiplicativeReduction.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.hasMultiplicativeReduction_baseChange_quadraticTwistOf
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: hasMultiplicativeReduction_baseChange_quadraticTwistOf
-- L4DEL: intuition: **The twist by a unit discriminant keeps multiplicative reduction.** If `E` has multiplicative reduction and `D = t² - 4n` is a unit of `R` (residue `≠ 0`), then the base change of the `R`-model twist `(E.integralModel R).quadraticTwistOf t n` again has multiplicative reduction: its `c₄ = D² · c₄` is a unit (so the model is minimal and the reduction multiplicative) and its `Δ = D⁶ · Δ` still has positive valuation.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/SplitMultiplicativeReduction.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.hasSplitMultiplicativeReduction_quadraticTwistOf_of_residue
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: hasSplitMultiplicativeReduction_quadraticTwistOf_of_residue
-- L4DEL: intuition: Packaging `nodePoly_quadraticTwistOf_map_splits_of_residue`: if the base change of the twisted integral model has multiplicative reduction and the residues of `(t', n')` satisfy the trace and norm relations, then the reduction is *split* multiplicative.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/SplitMultiplicativeReduction.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.nodePoly_map_root_relations
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: nodePoly_map_root_relations
-- L4DEL: intuition: If the root of the reduced node polynomial `P̄` (assumed irreducible) satisfies a monic quadratic relation `X² - t·X + n` over the residue field, then comparing with the defining relation of `P̄` (`aeval_root_nodePoly_map`) and using the linear independence of `1` and the root (`AdjoinRoot.eq_zero_of_mul_root_add_eq_zero`) yields the relations `φc₄·t + φ(a₁c₄) = 0` and `φc₄·n + φκ = 0` (φ = residue, `κ = 54b₆ - 3b₂b₄ + a₂c₄`).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/SplitMultiplicativeReduction.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.nodePoly_quadraticTwistOf_map_splits_iff
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: nodePoly_quadraticTwistOf_map_splits_iff
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Twisting flips the square class (residue characteristic ≠ 2).** Combining the split criterion `nodePoly_map_splits_iff_isSquare` with the coefficient scaling of the quadratic twist (`c₄_quadraticTwistOf`, `c₆_quadraticTwistOf`), the node polynomial of `W.quadraticTwistOf t n` splits over a field `k` of characteristic `≠ 2` exactly when `D · (-c₄ c₆)` is a square there, where `D = t² - 4n`. Thus twisting multiplies the square class governing splitting by `D`: it converts a nonsplit reduction into a split one precisely when `D` and `-c₄ c₆` lie in the same square class.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/SplitMultiplicativeReduction.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.nodePoly_quadraticTwistOf_map_splits_of_residue
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: nodePoly_quadraticTwistOf_map_splits_of_residue
-- L4DEL: intuition: If the residues of `(t', n')` satisfy the trace and norm relations cut out by the node polynomial, then the node polynomial of the quadratic twist of the integral model by `(t', n')` splits over the residue field: the key identity `φc₄ · φ(t'² - 4n') = -φc₆` (`residue_c₄_mul_residue_eq_neg_c₆`) reduces this to a square-class computation for residue characteristic `≠ 2`, and to an Artin–Schreier computation for residue characteristic `2` (`nodePoly_quadraticTwistOf_map_splits_of_residue_of_two_eq_zero`).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/SplitMultiplicativeReduction.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.nodePoly_quadraticTwistOf_map_splits_of_residue_of_two_eq_zero
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: nodePoly_quadraticTwistOf_map_splits_of_residue_of_two_eq_zero
-- L4DEL: intuition: The residue characteristic `2` case of `nodePoly_quadraticTwistOf_map_splits_of_residue`: the Artin–Schreier split condition (`nodePoly_map_splits_iff_of_two_eq_zero`) holds with `z = 0`, because `φ κ_W = 0`. Indeed `κ_W = D³κ - D²·n·a₁²·c₄` (`kappa_quadraticTwistOf`), and `φκ = -φc₄·φn` (`hB`), `φa₁ = -φt'` (`hA`), `φD = φt'²` (as `4 = 0`), so `φκ_W = -φD²·φc₄·φn·(φD + φa₁²) = -φD²·φc₄·φn·(2·φt'²) = 0`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/SplitMultiplicativeReduction.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.notMem_range_algebraMap_of_residue_notMem
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: notMem_range_algebraMap_of_residue_notMem
-- L4DEL: intuition: If the residue of an integral element `θ` of `S` does not come from the residue field of `R`, then `θ` does not come from `K` either: an element of `K` integral over the integrally closed `R` lies in `R`, and residues are compatible.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/SplitMultiplicativeReduction.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.residue_c₄_mul_residue_eq_neg_c₆
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: residue_c₄_mul_residue_eq_neg_c₆
-- L4DEL: intuition: The key identity `φc₄ · φ(t'² - 4n') = -φc₆` of the twisting datum `(t', n')`: if its residues satisfy the trace and norm relations cut out by the node polynomial (`κ = 54 b₆ - 3 b₂ b₄ + a₂ c₄`), then the discriminant identity `splitPolynomial_discrim` turns them into this identity.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/QuadraticTwists/SplitMultiplicativeReduction.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.Δ_baseChange_quadraticTwistOf_ne_zero
-- L4DEL: title: The base change of the twisted integral model has nonzero discriminant: its `Δ` is `(t'² - 4n')⁶ · Δ` (`Δ_quadraticTwistOf`), and both factors are nonzero
-- L4DEL: intuition: The base change of the twisted integral model has nonzero discriminant: its `Δ` is `(t'² - 4n')⁶ · Δ` (`Δ_quadraticTwistOf`), and both factors are nonzero.
