@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/TateCurve.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.exists_variableChange_tateCurve
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: exists_variableChange_tateCurve
-- L4DEL: intuition: Tate's theorem (Silverman, ATAEC V.5.3): an elliptic curve with split multiplicative reduction is isomorphic, by a change of Weierstrass coordinates, to the Tate curve of its Tate parameter. Since `j(E)` is non-integral, `Aut` of the curve is `{±1}` and there are exactly *two* such `C`, differing by negation. `tateEquiv` is `tateCurveEquiv` transported along a choice of one of them; this binary choice, for each `E`, is the only choice in the whole theory, and it cannot be made functorially in `E` — see `tateEquiv_baseChange`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own leading source comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/TateCurve.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.one_lt_valuation_j
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: one_lt_valuation_j
-- L4DEL: intuition: An elliptic curve over `k` with split multiplicative reduction has non-integral `j`-invariant, `|j(E)| > 1`: indeed `v(j) = -v(Δ_min) < 0`, since `c₄` is a unit when the reduction is multiplicative.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/TateCurve.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.q
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: q
-- L4DEL: intuition: The Tate parameter of an elliptic curve `E`, given by a minimal Weierstrass equation with split multiplicative reduction over a nonarchimedean local field `k`: the unique element `q` of `k` with `0 < |q| < 1` such that `j(E) = j(q) = q⁻¹ + 744 + 196884q + ⋯`, defined directly (with no appeal to choice) as `tateParameter E.j`, the inverse `j`-series evaluated at `j(E)`. Equivalently, the unique `q` such that `E(k̄)` is Galois-equivariantly isomorphic to `k̄ˣ/q^ℤ`. (The bare existence of an abstract isomorphism `E(k) ≅ kˣ/q^ℤ` would not pin down `q`: already over `ℚ_p` the groups `ℚ_pˣ/p^ℤ` and `ℚ_pˣ/(p(1+p))^ℤ` are isomorphic, even topologically.)
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/TateCurve.lean
