@decl TateCurve.a₆
-- L4DEL: title: The coefficient `a₆ = -(5s₃ + 7s₅)/12 = -q - 23q² - ⋯` of the Tate curve `y² + xy = x³ + a₄x + a₆`
-- L4DEL: intuition: The coefficient `a₆ = -(5s₃ + 7s₅)/12 = -q - 23q² - ⋯` of the Tate curve `y² + xy = x³ + a₄x + a₆`. (Division by `12` is implemented as scalar multiplication by `12⁻¹ ∈ ℚ(u)`; note that `5σ₃(n) + 7σ₅(n)` is always divisible by `12`, so `a₆` in fact has integer coefficients, though we do not need this.)
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/TateCurveConstruction.lean
@decl TateCurve.Blueprint.analytic_weierstrass
-- L4DEL: title: The analytic form of the main theorem (Silverman, *Advanced topics*, Theorem V.1.1(a)): for `0 < ‖q‖ < ‖u‖ < 1`, `Yₐ² + XₐYₐ = Xₐ³ - 5s₃(q)Xₐ - (5s₃(q) + 7s₅(q))/12`
-- L4DEL: intuition: The analytic form of the main theorem (Silverman, *Advanced topics*, Theorem V.1.1(a)): for `0 < ‖q‖ < ‖u‖ < 1`, `Yₐ² + XₐYₐ = Xₐ³ - 5s₃(q)Xₐ - (5s₃(q) + 7s₅(q))/12`. Proof sketch: the hypotheses ensure `u ∉ qᶻ`, and we may choose `z`, `τ` with `e z = u`, `e τ = q`, `0 < im z < im τ` (so `z ∉ Λ_τ`). Substitute the four `q`-expansions into the differential equation `℘'² = 4℘³ - g₂℘ - g₃` (`PeriodPair.derivWeierstrassP_sq`) and divide by `(2πi)⁶` and by `4` (`analytic_weierstrass_algebra`).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/TateCurveConstruction.lean
@decl TateCurve.Blueprint.corrector_row_eval
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: corrector_row_eval
-- L4DEL: intuition: The corrector rows of the `℘`-expansion: `∑_{m : ℤ} (nτ + m)⁻² = (2πi)² V(qⁿ) + [n = 0]π²/3` where `V(v) = v/(1-v)²` (the row `n = 0` is the Basel problem, and its `V`-term is junk `0`).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/TateCurveConstruction.lean
@decl TateCurve.Blueprint.derivWeierstrassP_q_expansion
-- L4DEL: title: The `q`-expansion of `℘'` (Silverman, *Advanced topics*, Theorem I.6.2): under the hypotheses of `weierstrassP_q_expansion`, `℘'(z; Λ_τ) = (2πi)³ (Xₐ(e z, e τ) + 2Yₐ(e z, e τ))`
-- L4DEL: intuition: The `q`-expansion of `℘'` (Silverman, *Advanced topics*, Theorem I.6.2): under the hypotheses of `weierstrassP_q_expansion`, `℘'(z; Λ_τ) = (2πi)³ (Xₐ(e z, e τ) + 2Yₐ(e z, e τ))`. Proof: as for `weierstrassP_q_expansion`, but simpler: group the absolutely convergent sum `℘'(z) = -2∑_ω (z - ω)⁻³` into rows `ω = nτ + m` (no regularising terms are needed here) and apply `sum_int_inv_cube'` to each row. The identity `v/(1-v)² + 2v²/(1-v)³ = v(1+v)/(1-v)³` recombines the result into `Xₐ + 2Yₐ`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/TateCurveConstruction.lean
