@decl TateCurve.Blueprint.G_q_expansion
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: G_q_expansion
-- L4DEL: intuition: Common core of `g₂_q_expansion` and `g₃_q_expansion`: the `q`-expansion of the Eisenstein lattice sum `G k = ∑_{ω ∈ Λ_τ} ω⁻ᵏ` for even `k > 2`, given the row-sum identity with constant `C` (which is `(2πi)ᵏ/(k-1)!`) and the value `Z` of the row `n = 0` (which is `2ζ(k)`). Reindex the lattice sum by `ℤ × ℤ`, evaluate the rows (`row_eval_ne_zero`), and sum over `n` by Fubini and the divisor-sum rearrangement (`tsum_int_lambert_natAbs`), giving the coefficients `σⱼ`, `j = k - 1`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/TateCurveConstruction.lean
@decl TateCurve.Blueprint.g₂_q_expansion
-- L4DEL: title: The `q`-expansion of `g₂` (Silverman, *Advanced topics*, Theorem I.7.1): `g₂(Λ_τ) = (2πi)⁴/12 ⬝ (1 + 240s₃(q))`
-- L4DEL: intuition: The `q`-expansion of `g₂` (Silverman, *Advanced topics*, Theorem I.7.1): `g₂(Λ_τ) = (2πi)⁴/12 ⬝ (1 + 240s₃(q))`. This is `g₂ = 60G₄` and the case `k = 4` of `G_q_expansion`, with row-sum identity `sum_int_inv_fourth` and `2ζ(4) = π⁴/45` (`hasSum_int_inv_fourth`).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

