@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/TateCurveConstruction.lean
@decl TateCurve.Blueprint.hasSum_X_eval
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: hasSum_X_eval
-- L4DEL: intuition: Rearrangement for `X` (extracted from Silverman's proof of *Advanced topics*, Theorem V.3.1(c)): for `0 < ‖q‖ < ‖u‖ < 1` with `u` transcendental (so that evaluation of coefficients at `u` is a ring homomorphism), the coefficients of the formal series `TateCurve.X` evaluated at `u` sum to `Xₐ(u, q)`. Proof: expand each term of `Xₐ`: for `n ≥ 1`, `qⁿu/(1 - qⁿu)² = ∑_{m ≥ 1} m qⁿᵐuᵐ` (geometric series, `‖qⁿu‖ < 1`); for `n ≤ -1` with `N = -n`, the rational-function identity `v/(1-v)² = v⁻¹/(1-v⁻¹)²` gives `qⁿu/(1 - qⁿu)² = ∑_{m ≥ 1} m qᴺᵐu⁻ᵐ` (`‖qᴺu⁻¹‖ < 1`); and `-2s₁(q) = -2∑_N (∑_{d ∣ N} d) qᴺ`. All double series converge absolutely (`summable_coeff_prod`), so they may be collected by powers of `q` (`hasSum_divisor_collect`), and the coefficient of `qᴺ` is exactly `∑_{d ∣ N} d(uᵈ + u⁻ᵈ - 2)`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/TateCurveConstruction.lean
@decl TateCurve.Blueprint.hasSum_Y_eval
-- L4DEL: title: Rearrangement for `Y`: for `0 < ‖q‖ < ‖u‖ < 1` with `u` transcendental, the coefficients of the formal series `TateCurve.Y` evaluated at `u` sum to `Yₐ(u, q)`
-- L4DEL: intuition: Rearrangement for `Y`: for `0 < ‖q‖ < ‖u‖ < 1` with `u` transcendental, the coefficients of the formal series `TateCurve.Y` evaluated at `u` sum to `Yₐ(u, q)`. Proof: as for `hasSum_X_eval`, using `v²/(1-v)³ = ∑_{m ≥ 1} (m choose 2) vᵐ` for the rows `n ≥ 1`, the rational-function identity `v²/(1-v)³ = -v⁻¹/(1-v⁻¹)³` together with `v/(1-v)³ = ∑_{m ≥ 1} ((m+1) choose 2) vᵐ` for the rows `n ≤ -1`, and `s₁(q) = ∑_N (∑_{d ∣ N} d) qᴺ` for the correction term.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

