@decl TateCurve.valuation_evalInt_eq
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: valuation_evalInt_eq
-- L4DEL: intuition: The leading-term principle: if `F = X + O(X²)` then `|F(q)| = |q|` on the punctured open unit disc — ultrametrically the leading term dominates the tail, which has valuation at most `|q|²` by `valuation_evalInt_le_pow`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/TateParameter.lean
@decl TateCurve.valuation_evalInt_le_pow
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: valuation_evalInt_le_pow
-- L4DEL: intuition: If the first `M` coefficients of `F` vanish, its evaluation at a point of the open unit disc has valuation at most `|q|^M`: the partial sums satisfy the bound by the nonarchimedean triangle inequality, and it passes to the limit by the ultrametric isosceles principle (if `v(σ - T) < v(T)` and `v(σ) < v(T)` then `v(T) ≤ max(v(σ), v(σ - T)) < v(T)`, absurd).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/TateParameter.lean
@decl TateCurve.ΔFormal
-- L4DEL: title: The formal discriminant `Δ(q) = q∏_{n≥1}(1 - qⁿ)²⁴ ∈ ℤ⟦q⟧` of the Tate curve: the `q`-expansion of the modular discriminant, `∑ τ(n)qⁿ` with `τ` Ramanujan's tau
-- L4DEL: intuition: The formal discriminant `Δ(q) = q∏_{n≥1}(1 - qⁿ)²⁴ ∈ ℤ⟦q⟧` of the Tate curve: the `q`-expansion of the modular discriminant, `∑ τ(n)qⁿ` with `τ` Ramanujan's tau. The product is a `tprod` in the `X`-adic topology, multipliable by `PowerSeries.WithPiTopology.multipliable_one_sub_X_pow`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/KnownIn1980s/EllipticCurves/TateParameter.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.tateParameter
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: tateParameter
-- L4DEL: intuition: The inverse of `q ↦ j(q)` (Silverman, ATAEC V.5.2): for `|j| > 1`, the unique `q` with `0 < |q| < 1` and `j(q) = j`, namely the evaluation `q = j⁻¹ + 744j⁻² + 750420j⁻³ + ⋯` at `j⁻¹` of the integral power series `TateCurve.jInvReverse`. Over a field complete with respect to a rank 1 nonarchimedean valuation the series converges for `|j| > 1`, since its coefficients are integers, hence of norm `≤ 1` (junk value for `|j| ≤ 1`). Design note: unique existence cannot be turned into data in Lean without `Classical.choose`, so instead of stating V.5.2 as an `∃!` we take the *inverse map* — an explicit power series, hence data on its own merits — as the definition. Uniqueness is then a round-trip identity (`tateParameter (j(q)) = q`, future work), and no choice is involved anywhere.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

