@file FLT/Mathlib/AlgebraicGeometry/EllipticCurve/Reduction.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.isMinimal_of_valuation_c₄_eq_one
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: isMinimal_of_valuation_c₄_eq_one
-- L4DEL: intuition: **Minimality criterion.** An integral Weierstrass equation over `K` whose `c₄` is a unit of `R` (equivalently, `valuation c₄ = 1`) is already minimal: any change of variables `C` keeping the equation integral must have `valuation C.u ≥ 1` (else `valuation (C • W).c₄ = valuation C.u⁻⁴ > 1`, contradicting integrality), so `valuation (C • W).Δ = valuation C.u⁻¹² · valuation W.Δ ≤ valuation W.Δ`. This is the tool that shows the twist `W` we build is minimal without minimising by hand. This is the unit-`c₄` case of the Kraus–Laska criterion: the special case "`v(c₄) < 4` or `v(Δ) < 12` implies minimal" of Silverman, *The Arithmetic of Elliptic Curves*, Remark VII.1.1, restricted to `v(c₄) = 0`. The hypothesis is stated at the field level — via the adic valuation of `W.c₄ : K` — to match mathlib's phrasing of `WeierstrassCurve.HasMultiplicativeReduction`, whose `multiplicativeReduction` field is exactly `(maximalIdeal R).valuation K W.c₄ = 1`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/Mathlib/AlgebraicGeometry/EllipticCurve/Reduction.lean
@decl WeierstrassCurve.map_nodePoly
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: map_nodePoly
