@file FLT/Mathlib/FieldTheory/Galois/Basic.lean
@decl quadraticCharacter
-- L4DEL: title: The quadratic character of `Aut(M/K)` attached to a separable quadratic subextension `K ⊆ L ⊆ M`: it sends `σ` to `1` if `σ` fixes `L` pointwise, and to `-1` otherwise
-- L4DEL: intuition: The quadratic character of `Aut(M/K)` attached to a separable quadratic subextension `K ⊆ L ⊆ M`: it sends `σ` to `1` if `σ` fixes `L` pointwise, and to `-1` otherwise. Since `L/K` is normal, every `K`-automorphism of `M` maps `L` to `L`, and multiplicativity (`map_mul'`) follows because restriction to `L` is a homomorphism to the order 2 group `Gal(L/K)`. Taking `M = L` gives the quadratic character of `Gal(L/K)` itself; if `M/K` is normal then this character is the composite of the restriction `Aut(M/K) → Gal(L/K)` with the unique isomorphism `Gal(L/K) ≃ {±1}`, and in particular is surjective (`quadraticCharacter_surjective`).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/Mathlib/FieldTheory/Galois/Basic.lean
@decl quadraticCharacter_surjective
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: quadraticCharacter_surjective
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `M/K` is normal (for example `M = L`, or `M` a separable closure of `K`) then the nontrivial element of `Gal(L/K)` extends to an automorphism of `M`, so the quadratic character of `Aut(M/K)` attached to `L/K` is surjective.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

