@file FLT/Mathlib/RingTheory/DiscreteValuationRing/AdjoinRoot.lean
@decl IsLocalRing.of_isMaximal_map_maximalIdeal
-- L4DEL: title: Documented declaration: of_isMaximal_map_maximalIdeal
-- L4DEL: intuition: An integral algebra `S` over a local ring `R` such that `𝔪_R · S` is a maximal ideal is itself local, with maximal ideal `𝔪_R · S`: any maximal ideal of `S` contracts to `𝔪_R` by integrality, hence contains `𝔪_R · S`, hence equals it.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/Mathlib/RingTheory/DiscreteValuationRing/Separable.lean
@decl Polynomial.Monic.separable_map_algebraMap_of_separable_map_residue
-- L4DEL: title: A monic polynomial over a discrete valuation ring `R` whose reduction to the residue field is separable stays separable over the fraction field `K`
-- L4DEL: intuition: A monic polynomial over a discrete valuation ring `R` whose reduction to the residue field is separable stays separable over the fraction field `K`. Proof: if `P.map (algebraMap R K)` is not separable, it has a monic common factor `g` of positive degree with its derivative. Since `R` is integrally closed and `g` is a monic divisor of the monic `P`, Gauss's lemma descends `g` to a monic `Q ∈ R[X]`, which then divides `P` and `derivative P` already in `R[X]` (`map_dvd_map`). Reducing mod `𝔪_R`, the reduction of `Q` is a common factor of positive degree of `P̄` and `derivative P̄`, contradicting `P̄.Separable`.
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

@file FLT/Mathlib/RingTheory/Localization/BaseChange.lean
@decl IsLocalization.leftModuleTensorEquiv
-- L4DEL: title: If `A` is a localization of R, tensoring two A-modules over A is the same as tensoring them over R
-- L4DEL: intuition: If `A` is a localization of R, tensoring two A-modules over A is the same as tensoring them over R. This is `IsLocalization.moduleTensorEquiv` as an `M₁`-linear equivalence (more generally an `M'`-linear equivalence where `M₁` is an `M'`-module).
-- L4DEL: step: This explanation is a condensed rendering of the declaration's own doc comment at the pinned source commit; consult the exact Lean statement below for authority.

